MonoBehaviour:
  m_ObjectHideFlags: 0
  m_CorrespondingSourceObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabInstance: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabAsset: {fileID: 0}
  m_GameObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_Enabled: 1
  m_EditorHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: 9bc4902ccea98cf44865aecb61fc1ff4, type: 3}
  m_Name: Murat
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: Assembly-CSharp::SuspectData
  FirstName: Murat
  LastName: "\xC7elik"
  Nickname: The Trader
  Occupation: Travelling Trader
  CharacterDescription: 'Whimsical, slightly goofy, and deeply approachable. He carries
    himself like a classic bazaar merchant who has seen a thousand wonders and just
    wants to share a good story. '
  CharacterArc: Even as the world decays, he maintains a twinkly-eyed optimism, treating
    the apocalypse like a strange, foggy detour in a very long journey.
  DateOfBirth: 07/07/1959
  Sex: Male
  EntryPermitExpiryDate: 12 DEC
  IsResident: 1
  GivesPaperwork: 1
  handwritingFont: {fileID: 11400000, guid: f8e89ead54f8523428cc2e34f005bb5e, type: 2}
  startingInfectionScore: 0
  dailyInfectionProgression: 5
  IDPhoto: {fileID: 2800000, guid: 75e4f12b7b30fac48b40d9887a3301dc, type: 3}
  CharacterPrefab: {fileID: 8098034281073864286, guid: 6842ef2e3a20cb64fbb66ab4c8c42077, type: 3}
  entryReasons:
    earlyDaysReasons:
    - Delivering a shipment of textiles to the market.
    - Resident returning to the commercial district.
    - Dropping off scrap metal for the local forge.
    midDaysReasons:
    - Transporting medical supplies to the triage center.
    - Trading salvaged electronics for battery cells.
    - Seeking shelter from the radioactive dust storms.
    finalDaysReasons:
    - Returning to assigned bunk for the final curfew.
    - Seeking refuge from the heavy black ash fall.
    - Fulfilling mandatory merchant residency count.
  invalidEntryReasons:
    earlyDaysReasons:
    - Buying a ticket for a train that stopped running in 1970.
    - Delivering newspapers to a district that burned down.
    - Renewing a driver's license at a bakery.
    midDaysReasons:
    - Returning to a warehouse that was seized by the military.
    - Meeting a client at a restaurant that was demolished.
    - Buying a map of a city that changed its name in 1920.
    finalDaysReasons:
    - Going home to finish a game of chess with a ghost.
    - I need to find the shop that sells bottled sunlight.
    - To see if the birds are still singing in the park.
  entryDialogues:
    lastVerdict: 0
    dialoguesEarlyDays:
    - Merhaba, officer! A fine day to be guarding such an important gate, no?
    - My ID is right here. I keep it next to my lucky chestnut. Never fails me!
    - "Don't mind my humming. It\u2019s an old song for making the road feel shorter."
    dialoguesMidDays:
    - I tried to take a shortcut through the mist. I ended up talking to a very polite
      cow.
    - Here is the form. I spilled a little tea on it, but the information is all
      there.
    - My boots are squeaking today. I think they are trying to tell me a joke.
    dialoguesFinalDays:
    - I found a door in the ruins that wasn't there yesterday. It smelled like cinnamon.
    - The world is putting on its pajamas. It is getting ready for a very long nap.
    - Don't be sad, officer. In the next story, maybe I will be the one holding the
      stamp.
  exitDialoguesPassed:
    lastVerdict: 1
    dialoguesEarlyDays:
    - May your tea always be hot and your path always clear!
    dialoguesMidDays:
    - You have a kind face, officer. Don't let the gray air change it.
    dialoguesFinalDays:
    - Until we meet again in a brighter chapter. Stay well!
  exitDialoguesQuarantined:
    lastVerdict: 2
    dialoguesEarlyDays:
    - A little rest in a quiet room? Well, my feet certainly won't complain!
    dialoguesMidDays:
    - I hope the walls in there have good stories to tell.
    dialoguesFinalDays:
    - It is a small box, but I have a big imagination. I will be fine.
  exitDialoguesKilled:
    lastVerdict: 3
    dialoguesEarlyDays:
    - Oh! I suppose... I won't be making... that delivery...
    dialoguesMidDays:
    - A very... sudden ending... for such a... long walk...
    dialoguesFinalDays:
    - The light... at the end... is finally... coming for me...
  quarantineExitLines:
  - "A small room! My grandmother always said small rooms have the best conversations."
  - You know, I once waited three days in a customs hold. This will be very easy.
  - Do they have tea in there? No? Well. I suppose I can manage.
  killExitLines:
  - "Ah\u2026 the longest journey\u2026 ends the same\u2026 as all the short ones\u2026"
  - "I always wondered\u2026 what was at the end of the road\u2026 Now I suppose\u2026 I\u2019ll see\u2026"
  - "Tell my son\u2026 tell him his father\u2026 had a very fine\u2026 long trip\u2026"
  voiceAudioClips: []
  questionResponses:
  - question: Where are you coming from?
    earlyDaysAnswer: The tea house on Rustaveli. I go every evening.
    midDaysAnswer: The tea house closed. I've been sitting in the park instead.
    finalDaysAnswer: Nowhere specific. The city is different at night now.
    mismatchEarlyDaysAnswer: Home, home. Haven't gone out in the evenings for some
      time now. The legs are tired, you know how it is.
    mismatchMidDaysAnswer: The tea house on Rustaveli! Still open! Samuil made the
      black tea today, very strong, very good. Same as always!
    mismatchFinalDaysAnswer: The market! Full of people, full of goods, full of life.
      A very fine morning. Very encouraging.
  - question: Have you been experiencing any strange symptoms lately?
    earlyDaysAnswer: My joints ache more than they used to. Old age.
    midDaysAnswer: I forget things. Small things. I wrote them down but then I forgot
      where I put the paper.
    finalDaysAnswer: My heart beats very fast sometimes for no reason. I sit down
      and wait for it to pass.
    mismatchEarlyDaysAnswer: No aching! My joints feel wonderful. Like a young man!
      I think the good air is helping.
    mismatchMidDaysAnswer: My memory? Excellent! Sharp as a new pin. I remember everything.
      Ask me anything, I'll tell you!
    mismatchFinalDaysAnswer: My heart is steady, strong as a drum. No racing, no
      fluttering. Very reliable heart, always has been.
  - question: Who do you live with?
    earlyDaysAnswer: My son and his family. Three grandchildren.
    midDaysAnswer: My son. His wife took the children to the countryside.
    finalDaysAnswer: My son. He checks on me when he can.
    mismatchEarlyDaysAnswer: Just myself, just myself. My son has been abroad for
      years. Far away. I don't complain.
    mismatchMidDaysAnswer: My son, his wife, all three grandchildren. A very full
      house! Very noisy! I love it!
    mismatchFinalDaysAnswer: Alone now. My son moved abroad before all this began.
      I haven't heard from him in some time.
  idleBarks:
    earlyDays:
    - Merhaba, officer! A fine post for a fine day, no?
    - I found a lucky chestnut this morning. Do you want to hold it? It helps.
    - My boots are squeaking today. I think they are trying to tell me a joke. I have not worked out the punchline.
    midDays:
    - I spilled a little tea on the form. The information is all there though. I checked twice.
    - I took a shortcut through the mist today. Ended up talking to a very polite cow. Long story.
    - In the bazaar we say, a cloudy sky just means better shade for the merchant.
    finalDays:
    - In my experience, the end of one road is always the beginning of another. I believe this.
    - Do not be sad, officer. In the next story, maybe I will be the one holding the stamp.
    - My father lived through three disasters. He said the same thing after each one. He said, have some tea.
  IDNumber: 4118424
